// WHAT THE LAYERS HOLD, LAID OUT FOR THEIR WIDTH (layoutFloor.test.ts, lot 32): the
// layers whose content can run past their edge - a select as wide as its value, a row
// that does not wrap -, as the running app rendered them on a phone held sideways
// (740x360) and upright (360x740): the scheduled task editor with a cron expression and
// its time zone; the same editor every N units; the same editor once at a date; the date
// picker it opens; an instance's agents, one of them reserved for a group with its
// actions; the people picker of a conversation with its arrival role; a group's members;
// a group's charts with the select that adds one; a bridge's configuration; a
// conversation's people with a member's role; a conversation's agents, two of them
// deleted from their gateway and one the viewer may not address (lot 34: a new bench,
// its agents made up); the Talk settings. And the prompt
// injections page on the narrowest phone the guard knows, a Galaxy Fold folded
// (280x653), where its content locale select, a card's title and a placeholder's chip
// ran past the page. Their text is filler of the same length, its spaces, hyphens and
// slashes kept; nothing of a conversation or a person is kept. Captured with the audit
// harness (l32Save); the boxes that now hold a conversation's names and an agent's type
// labels were added to the captures as the components render them. Recapture when their
// markup changes - the guard checks that every class it relies on is still rendered by
// the components, and that every select trigger and every dialog carries its shared
// component's classes.

